# Beat-Field Tarraxo

**Ingredients:** TWIN ETHIOPIAN WASHINT FLUTES + TARRAXO + PREPARED VIBRAPHONE + KEbero RIM / WOODBLOCK + BASS TROMBONE + SHORT MONO SUB

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a dry-season residency in a disused grain-grading hall outside Addis Ababa, where two washint players, Hana Bekele and Sami Tesfaye, were testing near-unison long tones across the empty concrete room while Lisbon producer Rui Matos built slow tarraxo patterns on a compact sampler. The crucial discovery was acoustic rather than melodic: when the two flutes held almost the same pitch but differed by only a few cents, the combined tone developed slow natural beating, an audible pulse created by interference between the two sound waves. Rui stopped adding tremolo and modulation effects and instead built the electronic groove around the speed of those natural beats. Prepared vibraphone, kebero rim percussion, woodblock, bass trombone, and a short mono sub completed a style where microscopic tuning differences create a second rhythm inside an already sparse club pulse.

Beat-Field Tarraxo is a low-tempo acoustic-electronic dance form built around near-unison interference. Tarraxo supplies the chassis: 88–102 BPM, low compact kick, clipped rim or clap, sparse syncopation, short sub-bass punctuation, long gaps, and a body-heavy sense of pulse that depends more on placement than density. Twin washints supply the acoustic engine through paired sustained notes, breath texture, narrow intonation offsets, alternate fingering color, octave overblow, short grace notes, and controlled drift around a shared tonal center.

The two washints are recorded separately enough to preserve individual breath and fingering detail but close enough in the stereo field that their interaction remains obvious. Each flute receives a small-diaphragm condenser approximately 30–50 cm off axis from the embouchure to avoid excessive wind noise. A central ribbon microphone several meters away captures the combined interference field in the room. The individual close channels stay mostly dry; the room ribbon is used to reveal the slow rise and fall of acoustic beating.

Players favor tonic, fifth, octave, minor third, suspended second, and narrow pentatonic cells. The defining gesture is not a fast melody but a paired note held long enough for the listener to hear the amplitude beating emerge. One player may sit exactly on the reference pitch while the other moves two, four, six, or eight cents sharp or flat. The closer the two frequencies are, the slower the beating; a wider offset produces faster throbbing.

The central compositional device is the **Beat Field**. Every important twin-flute passage is assigned a target interference speed: SLOW, WALK, PULSE, or BLUR. SLOW uses extremely close tuning so the combined volume swells only once every several seconds. WALK increases the offset until the beating becomes a slow body pulse. PULSE creates a clearly rhythmic fluctuation that can interlock with the tarraxo grid. BLUR widens the detuning enough that the two flutes are heard as a rough double rather than a discrete beat.

A second device, **Zero-Line**, brings the two washints into near-perfect unison. The acoustic beating almost disappears, making the sound suddenly feel narrower and more stable without reducing volume. Zero-Line sections function as structural resets and are often paired with the sparsest drum pattern.

A third device, **Beat Handoff**, transfers rhythmic responsibility between acoustic interference and electronic percussion. When the washint beat field becomes faster and more obvious, hats, rims, and auxiliary percussion are removed. When the two flutes return toward Zero-Line, the electronic pattern becomes more active. The total perceived rhythmic density stays roughly constant even though its source changes.

A fourth device, **Phase Crossing**, lets one washint slowly pass through the pitch of the other. The beating accelerates as the pitches approach, appears to vanish at the crossing point, then returns as the drifting flute continues past the reference note. The electronic bass is forbidden from changing during the crossing; the acoustic event must remain the main source of motion.

Tarraxo drums stay minimal and close. Kick is short, low, and placed with deliberate asymmetry. Rim or clap is dry and often slightly late. Hats are rare. Small tom or percussion events appear only in low-beat-rate passages. The groove should feel heavy without becoming busy.

The short mono sub uses root, fifth, octave, and occasional flat-seventh notes with fast release. It enters as punctuation rather than as a continuous rolling bassline. During Phase Crossing, the sub holds a stable pitch so the tuning drift remains easy to perceive.

Prepared vibraphone provides cold harmonic landmarks. Selected bars are damped with removable felt or soft clips to shorten decay. Parts favor open fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, and two-note octave-spread shells. Vibraphone never doubles both washints in exact unison; it either confirms the shared reference note after a Beat Field settles or occupies a separate register.

Kebero is reduced to dry edge vocabulary: rim taps, muted slaps, fingertip rolls, and restrained low-head accents. Woodblock provides a narrow transient at changes between SLOW, WALK, PULSE, and BLUR. These acoustic percussion sounds replace selected programmed hits rather than becoming a folkloric parallel groove.

Bass trombone supplies low acoustic pressure through soft pedals, breath-heavy fundamentals, short falling fourths, tongue stops, and muted attacks. It is used mainly when the mono sub drops out, preventing the low register from becoming uniformly synthetic.

Vocals are optional and sparse. Amharic, Portuguese, or English fragments may reference grain grades, scale marks, parallel lines, tuning offsets, or instructions such as “hold the center,” “two cents high,” “cross slowly,” and “meet at zero.” Delivery should be spoken, close, and understated.

Effects remain restrained. Washints stay mostly dry with only a short room. No chorus, flanger, tremolo, or artificial stereo widening is allowed on the paired flute tones; the modulation must come from real tuning interference. Vibraphone receives a compact plate. Bass trombone gets a dark chamber. Drum bus compression is firm but not pumping. No cinematic Ethiopian strings, trance pads, EDM risers, long 808 glides, or synthetic flute doubling is allowed.

The defining tension is microscopic pitch difference versus massive physical groove. Two nearly identical notes create their own slow pulse, and the tarraxo rhythm deliberately makes room for that invisible acoustic geometry.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 88–102 BPM, sparse 4/4 tarraxo architecture with low compact kick, clipped rim/clap, minimal hats, short sub punctuation, and long twin-washint tones whose interference rate creates a second internal pulse.

**Harmonic Palette:** Pentatonic and modal centers, tonic-fifth anchors, suspended seconds, minor thirds, minor sevenths, octave reinforcement, near-unisons, and microtonal detuning offsets typically within 1–12 cents.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Beat Field** — twin washints hold near-unison tones at controlled tuning offsets to generate natural amplitude beating.
2. **Zero-Line** — both flutes converge into near-perfect unison so the beating collapses and the spectrum narrows.
3. **Beat Handoff** — electronic percussion density decreases as acoustic beat rate becomes more rhythmically obvious, then returns as the flutes stabilize.
4. **Phase Crossing** — one washint slowly crosses through the pitch of the other, causing beating to accelerate, disappear near unison, then reappear.
5. **Stable-Sub Rule** — mono sub remains harmonically fixed during major tuning drifts so the acoustic motion is perceptually clear.
6. **No-Artificial-Modulation Rule** — chorus, tremolo, flanging, and synthetic detune cannot imitate the central interference effect.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through wider microtonal offsets, faster acoustic beating, more forceful breath, denser tarraxo kick placement, firmer kebero rim accents, and stronger brass pedals. Climaxes remain low-slung and spacious rather than loud or layered.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Beat-Field Tarraxo formed inside a retired grain-grading and weighing hall outside Addis Ababa. The building retained long metal scales, yellowed inspection cards, numbered grain bins, burlap sacks, concrete sample tables, rusted ventilation louvres, mechanical balances, and faded painted lines showing acceptable grade tolerances.

Washint players **Hana Bekele** and **Sami Tesfaye** had been using the hall because the long dry room made tuning discrepancies immediately audible. During one rehearsal, Hana held a stable tonic while Sami attempted to match it. Instead of locking perfectly, he stayed slightly sharp. The room filled with a slow throbbing neither player was deliberately performing.

Lisbon producer **Rui Matos**, working in an adjacent office with sparse tarraxo sketches, heard the pulse through the wall and initially assumed it came from a tremolo pedal. When he realized the sound was entirely acoustic, he deleted several percussion tracks and matched the remaining electronic rhythm to the rate of the interference.

Prepared-vibraphone player **Marta Leal** joined after developing short harmonic checkpoints that could confirm the tonal center without eliminating the ambiguity between the two flutes. Percussionist **Yared Alemu** reduced his setup to one kebero edge, one woodblock, and one muted hand drum. Bass-trombonist **Nuno Vale** completed the group with low acoustic weight for sections where the mono sub disappears.

Their first performances were called **tolerance tests**. A narrow board behind the band showed a central line marked ZERO with small measurements on either side: ±2, ±4, ±6, ±8 cents. The board controlled nothing and used no tuner feed. It served only as a listening map for the audience.

The visual language developed around ochre dust, grain-sack beige, faded industrial green, scale-marker black, oxidized metal, chalk white, dry concrete grey, and deep burgundy. The unofficial rule is that if the main pulse can be recreated by muting either washint, the arrangement is not using the interference field strongly enough.

